Context from prior work:
Goal: Establish the initial Bun-based CLI entrypoint for TaskForge with a stable command surface and basic argument parsing.
Decisions: Use Bun as the runtime and set up a bin entry in package.json; Bootstrap with src/cli.ts and a modular structure: src/commands/*.ts and src/lib/args.ts; Implement a thin internal dispatcher for commands (help, version, and placeholders run/memory/config) without external parser dependencies; Return structured exit codes/messages for CLI misuse instead of throwing; Define a test plan covering no-args help, --help, version, known/unknown commands, and invalid flags
Risks: Overengineering the parser early could slow progress; keep the implementation minimal; Bin invocation semantics may differ between local Bun runs and installed binaries; requires smoke testing; Help/version output drift if commands are defined separately from a single source of truth; plan for synchronization
Snippets: #!/usr/bin/env bun
// src/cli.ts: minimal bootstrap for taskforge
const cmd = process.argv[2] ?? 'help';
switch (cmd) {
  case 'help':
  case '--help':
  case '-h':
    console.log('Usage: taskforge [command] [options]\n\nCommands: help, version, run, memory, config');
    break;
  case 'version':
  case '--version':
    console.log(require('./package.json').version);
    break;
  default:
    console.error(`Unknown command: ${cmd}`);
    process.exit(1);
}

